'''Salvatnet'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Fakta om stedsnavn |url=https://stadnamn.kartverket.no/fakta/136306 |access-date=2019-05-26 |website=kartverket.no |publisher=[[Kartverket]] |language=no}}</ref> is a [[lake]] in [[Namsos Municipality]] in [[Trøndelag]] [[counties of Norway|county]], [[Norway]]. With its deepest depth of {{convert|482|m}}, it is Norway's and [[Europe]]'s [[Lakes in Norway#Deepest lakes|second-deepest lake]], after [[Hornindalsvatnet]]. Alternate sources give the depth as either {{convert|464|m}} (old manual method) or {{convert|482|m}} (modern echo sounding method) at the deepest point. The lake sits close to the [[Norwegian Sea]], about {{convert|9|m}} above sea level at the surface and reaches to a depth of {{convert|473|m}} below sea level. It is a large lake with an area of {{convert|44.96|km2}}, a volume of {{convert|6.97|km3}}, and a shoreline that is {{convert|105.61|km}} around.<ref name="snl">{{Cite web |last=Store norske leksikon |author-link=Store norske leksikon |title=Salsvatnet |url=http://snl.no/Salsvatnet |access-date=2011-09-22 |language=no}}</ref>

Salvatnet is a [[meromictic lake]], meaning that the water is permanently stratified, often without [[oxygen]] in the lower depths ([[bottom water]]) due to [[density gradient]] and a lack of turnover. A meromictic lake often preserves records of the geologic past. The lower layer of the lake is highly [[Saline water|saline]] and as a result denser than the higher levels of water. Other meromictic lakes in Norway; with old seawater in the lower depths include [[Botnvatnet  (Saltdal)|Botnvatnet]], [[Kilevann]], [[Rossfjordvatnet]], [[Rørholtfjorden]] an arm of the lake [[Tokke (lake)|Tokke]], [[Rørhopvatnet]], [[Tronstadvann]], and [[Øvervatnet (Fauske)|Øvervatnet]].

